WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.320
AI agent security patterns are the engineering controls that keep model-driven automation bounded, understandable, and accountable.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.740
In Python, an agent is not just a model call.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:19.200
It is an application that may reason over context, retrieve data, remember prior interactions, call tools, and sometimes take actions in other systems.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:30.700
That means agent security is about the whole system around the model: identity, data access, credentials, tool permissions, storage, logging, monitoring, and the business workflow the agent can affect.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.000
It helps to distinguish common terms.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.340
A chatbot primarily responds to user messages.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.400
An assistant may combine conversation with retrieval or simple tools.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.420
A workflow agent may plan steps across systems.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.640
An autonomous loop may continue working until a stop condition is reached.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:52.180
A tool-calling application lets model output request specific functions.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.720
A traditional API service exposes deterministic application behavior.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:04.300
These categories overlap, but the risk increases as the system gains memory, tool access, autonomy, and write-capable actions.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:07.240
Agent security is not only model safety.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:21.940
Ordinary Python application security still applies: authentication, authorization, secret handling, network controls, input validation, output validation, safe error handling, dependency review, logging, and incident response.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:30.800
Agents add a new pressure point because natural-language content can influence data access, tool selection, external communication, or business actions.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:36.860
The model may sound confident, but confidence is not authorization and language is not a security boundary.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:42.120
Secure agents should be designed as controlled systems, not trusted coworkers with unlimited authority.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:53.620
The application should decide which tools exist, which users can use them, which data can be retrieved, which actions require approval, what gets logged, how failures are handled, and when work must stop.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.600
The pattern is model-assisted automation under application control.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:03.500
That posture preserves usefulness while reducing the chance that a generated plan turns into unsafe behavior.
